            4. What Makes a Radiator Heater Reliable
          </h2>

          <p>
            Oil-filled heaters are among the most durable space heaters, but construction details still matter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Sealed Steel Reservoir</h3>

          <p>
            The oil reservoir is the heart of the heater. De'Longhi lists a high-quality steel assembly with a multilayer, corrosion-resistant finish, which protects against rust in damp basements and garages.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Simple Controls</h3>

          <p>
            Fewer electronics mean fewer parts to fail. Knob-controlled models, like the De'Longhi EW7707CM, PELONIS and Amazon Basics, are the simplest. Digital models, like the DREO, add convenience at the cost of complexity.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Safety Cut-Offs</h3>

          <p>
            Reliable also means safe when something goes wrong. Look for a tip-over switch, overheat protection and an independent safety listing such as ETL, all of which every pick here includes in some form.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="room-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Size Room Can a 1500W Radiator Heater Heat?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 1500-watt heater is the maximum for a standard US household outlet, and it is a one-room heater.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Common Rule of Thumb</h3>

          <p>
            A widely used rule is about 10 watts per square foot for supplemental heat, which puts a 1500W heater at roughly 150 square feet. Well-insulated rooms do better; drafty or high-ceilinged rooms do worse.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ignore Whole-House Claims</h3>

          <p>
            Some listings, including PELONIS's, say a 1500W radiator heats a whole house. It cannot. Plan on one room per heater.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Smaller Rooms, Lower Wattage</h3>

          <p>
            For a small bedroom or office, the Comfort Zone's 1200W maximum, or the 600W and 900W settings on other models, is often enough and puts less load on the circuit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Does It Cost to Run a Radiator Heater?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Running cost is simple arithmetic, and it is the same for every 1500W electric heater regardless of type.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Formula</h3>

          <p>
            Multiply kilowatts by hours by your electricity rate. A heater running at 1500 watts uses 1.5 kWh per hour. At an example rate of 16 cents per kWh, that is 24 cents an hour. Check your own rate on your utility bill.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Real Costs Are Lower</h3>

          <p>
            The thermostat cycles the element off once the room is warm, and lower settings use 600 or 900 watts. ECO and ComfortTemp modes, on the DREO and De'Longhi, manage this automatically.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat One Room, Not the House</h3>

          <p>
            A radiator heater saves money when you turn the central heating down and heat only the room you are in. Running it alongside full central heat simply adds cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Radiator Heater Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Oil-filled heaters run cooler on the surface than many heaters, but they are still space heaters and need the same care.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plug Into the Wall</h3>

          <p>
            Plug space heaters directly into a wall outlet. Extension cords and power strips can overheat under a continuous 1500W load.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep Clearance</h3>

          <p>
            Keep curtains, bedding, furniture and paper at least three feet away, and never cover the heater or dry clothes on it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use the Safety Features</h3>

          <p>
            Tip-over switches, overheat cut-offs and child locks only help if they work. Place the heater on a flat, hard floor and keep it where it will not be knocked over.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-outage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Radiator Heaters and Power Outages
          </h2>

          <p>
            Radiator heaters are electric, so they stop working when the power goes out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Running One on Backup Power</h3>

          <p>
            A 1500W heater draws a lot of power. A generator can run one; a portable power station drains quickly. Our guide on whether <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-space-heater/">a power station can run a space heater</a> explains the numbers. Use the 600W setting to stretch backup power.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat That Lasts After the Power Cuts</h3>

          <p>
            The oil keeps radiating heat for a while after power is lost, which helps a little in a short outage, but it is no substitute for a real outage plan.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Outage Heating Options</h3>

          <p>
            For heat without electricity, see our guide to the <a href="/best-indoor-heater-for-power-outage/">best indoor heaters for power outages</a>, and for whole-home backup, our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tips" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How to Get the Most From a Radiator Heater
          </h2>

          <p>
            A few habits make a radiator heater warmer and cheaper to run.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Switch It On Early</h3>

          <p>
            Oil takes time to heat. Turn the heater on 20 to 30 minutes before you need the room warm, or use a timer like the DREO's.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Close the Door</h3>

          <p>
            A radiator heater warms one room. Closing the door keeps that heat where you are.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Let the Thermostat Work</h3>

          <p>
            Set the thermostat to a comfortable temperature rather than running on maximum. ECO and ComfortTemp modes do this for you.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Place It Under a Window</h3>

          <p>
            A heater near a cold window or outside wall offsets the draft, as long as curtains are kept well clear. Sealing that window with a <a href="/best-window-insulation-kit/">window insulation kit</a> stops the draft at the source, so the heater can cycle off more often.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bedrooms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. The Best Radiator Heater for a Bedroom
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bedrooms are where oil-filled heaters make the most sense, because silence and steady heat matter more than speed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Silence and Darkness</h3>

          <p>
            With no fan, every heater here is quiet. For light sleepers, the DREO adds mute and screen-off modes so there are no beeps or glowing display at night, while knob-controlled models like the De'Longhi EW7707CM and PELONIS have nothing to light up at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Timers for Waking and Sleeping</h3>

          <p>
            A timer lets the heater switch off after you fall asleep and on again before you wake. The DREO lists a 24-hour timer; with basic models, a plug-in timer rated for a 1500W heater does the same job.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Comfort Without Dry Air</h3>

          <p>
            De'Longhi says its radiators deliver gentle heat that minimizes dry air, with no fan to stir up dust, pollen and dander, which many people find more comfortable for sleeping than a fan heater.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="garages-basements" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Radiator Heaters for Garages, Basements and Workshops
          </h2>

          <p>
            Oil-filled heaters can work in garages and basements, with some caveats.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where They Work Well</h3>

          <p>
            A small, insulated workshop, home office in a converted garage or finished basement room suits a radiator heater well: it gives steady background heat while you work, without blowing sawdust around.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where They Struggle</h3>

          <p>
            A large, uninsulated two-car garage loses heat faster than any 1500W heater can replace it. For those spaces, see our guide to the <a href="/best-garage-heater/">best garage heaters</a>, which covers higher-output options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Freeze Protection</h3>

          <p>
            Both De'Longhi models switch on automatically below 41°F, which can help keep a cold room from freezing. A heater is not a substitute for proper pipe insulation, but the anti-freeze setting is a useful extra in an unheated space.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="controls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. Thermostats, Timers and Smart Controls Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            Controls are the biggest difference between the models here, and they affect comfort and running cost.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dial Thermostats</h3>

          <p>
            Knob thermostats, on the De'Longhi EW7707CM, PELONIS, Amazon Basics, COSTWAY and Comfort Zone, use relative settings rather than exact degrees. They are simple and durable; you find the right setting by trial and leave it there.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Digital Thermostats</h3>

          <p>
            Digital thermostats, like the DREO's, let you set an exact temperature and hold it with a sensor. That avoids swings between too warm and too cool and can trim running costs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Energy-Saving Modes</h3>

          <p>
            De'Longhi's ComfortTemp and ECO Plus and DREO's ECO mode automatically balance power and temperature. They are worth using, because they do what most people forget to do manually: turn the power down once the room is warm.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-storing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. Buying, Unboxing and Storing a Radiator Heater
          </h2>

          <p>
            A few practical points make the first winter and every one after it go smoothly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Let It Stand Before First Use</h3>

          <p>
            If the heater arrives cold or was shipped on its side, let it stand upright at room temperature for a while before switching it on, so the oil settles. Check the manual for the maker's guidance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Expect a Smell the First Time</h3>

          <p>
            New heaters often give off a faint smell the first time the element and paint heat up. Run it in a ventilated room for the first session; the smell should fade quickly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Storing It Between Winters</h3>

          <p>
            Store the heater upright in a dry place. Models with a cord wrap, like the COSTWAY, keep the cord tidy and protected. Before using it again, check the cord and plug for damage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Signs It Is Time to Replace It</h3>

          <p>
            A heater that leaks oil, has a damaged cord or plug, or shuts off repeatedly for no reason should be replaced. Sealed oil cannot be refilled, so a leak means the heater has reached the end of its life.
          </p>
        </div>

