          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting One Heater to Warm the Whole House</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1500W heater is a one-room heater, whatever a listing claims. Close the door and heat the room you are using, and turn the central heating down to actually save money.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging Into an Extension Cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A continuous 1500W load can overheat extension cords and power strips. Plug directly into a wall outlet, ideally on a circuit without other big loads.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Turning It On Only When You Are Already Cold</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oil-filled heaters are slow to warm. Switch on early or set a timer so the room is warm when you need it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running It on Maximum All the Time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The thermostat and lower settings are what keep running costs down. Set a comfortable temperature and let it cycle, or use ECO or ComfortTemp modes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Placing It Too Close to Fabric</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Curtains, bedding and furniture need at least three feet of clearance. Never dry clothes on a radiator heater.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

