            15. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best oil-filled radiator heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The De'Longhi EW7707CM is the best oil-filled radiator heater for most homes. It has a sealed steel oil reservoir that never needs refilling, a ComfortTemp energy-saving mode, a cooler surface design and an anti-freeze setting, with simple controls that leave little to go wrong. The DREO is best if you want a remote, timer and digital thermostat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the most reliable radiator heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Reliability comes from a sealed steel reservoir, simple controls and proper safety cut-offs. De'Longhi lists a high-quality steel body, a permanently sealed oil reservoir and a multilayer corrosion-resistant finish on its EW7707CM and Dragon models. Simple knob-controlled heaters generally have fewer parts to fail than digital ones.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are oil-filled radiator heaters safe to leave on overnight?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are among the safer types, because they have no exposed glowing element and run cooler on the surface. Choose one with tip-over and overheat protection, plug it straight into a wall outlet, and keep bedding and curtains at least three feet away. Follow the maker's instructions for unattended use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do oil-filled heaters use a lot of electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 1500W heater uses 1.5 kWh per hour at full power, the same as any 1500W electric heater. The thermostat and lower 600W or 900W settings reduce real use. They save money only when you heat one room and turn the central heating down.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do oil-filled radiator heaters take to heat up?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Longer than fan heaters, because the oil has to warm first. Expect noticeable warmth after several minutes and full output after around 20 to 30 minutes. A timer, like the DREO's, lets the room warm before you arrive.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you ever need to refill an oil-filled heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The oil is permanently sealed inside the fins and is never burned or used up. De'Longhi, Amazon Basics and COSTWAY all describe their oil as sealed with no refilling needed. A heater that leaks oil should be replaced, not refilled.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will an oil-filled heater work in a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, they need electricity. The oil keeps radiating some heat for a while after the power goes off. To run one during an outage you need a generator, as a portable power station drains quickly at 1500W; otherwise use a heater designed for outages.
              </p>
            </div>

